@hostname = https://127.0.0.1:7058
@tenantId = 1
@baseUrl = {{hostname}}/tenant/{{tenantId}}
@patientId = bulk-delete-http-patient
@observationId = bulk-delete-http-observation
@tagSystem = https://example.org/ignixa/samples
@tagCode = bulk-delete-http

# Use a disposable test tenant, never tenant 0 or production.
# These examples only delete the tagged sample IDs below, not the whole tenant.
# The seed PUTs create or replace those IDs. Change the IDs/tag for a separate run.
#
# Prerequisites:
# - SQL-backed FHIR storage for hard delete and history purge.
# - DurableTask:Provider=SqlServer (or AzureStorage), NOT FileSystem.
# - BackgroundJobs:Repository=SqlServer for persistent job status.
# - If authorization is enabled, add your Authorization header to every request;
#   use a test principal with create/update/read/delete/search on the sample types
#   and wildcard search/delete for system kickoff, polling, and cancellation.
#
# Run the three seed PUTs, then ONE scenario at a time. Poll its named request's
# Content-Location until it stops returning 202 before verifying the result.
# Re-run the seed PUTs before trying another scenario. Do not use "Send All".
# Named-response variables work with VS Code REST Client / Visual Studio HTTP files.
# Alternatively, copy the returned Content-Location URL into a GET or DELETE request.
# In single-tenant mode, set baseUrl to {{hostname}} to test tenant-agnostic routes.

### Seed 1: create the sample Patient
PUT {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}
Content-Type: application/fhir+json

{
  "resourceType": "Patient",
  "id": "{{patientId}}",
  "meta": {
    "tag": [{ "system": "{{tagSystem}}", "code": "{{tagCode}}" }]
  },
  "active": true,
  "name": [{ "family": "BulkDeleteSample", "given": ["Test"] }]
}

### Seed 2: create another Patient version for the history-purge scenario
PUT {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}
Content-Type: application/fhir+json

{
  "resourceType": "Patient",
  "id": "{{patientId}}",
  "meta": {
    "tag": [{ "system": "{{tagSystem}}", "code": "{{tagCode}}" }]
  },
  "active": false,
  "name": [{ "family": "BulkDeleteSample", "given": ["Test"] }]
}

### Seed 3: create a sample Observation referencing the Patient
PUT {{baseUrl}}/Observation/{{observationId}}
Content-Type: application/fhir+json

{
  "resourceType": "Observation",
  "id": "{{observationId}}",
  "meta": {
    "tag": [{ "system": "{{tagSystem}}", "code": "{{tagCode}}" }]
  },
  "status": "final",
  "code": {
    "coding": [{ "system": "http://loinc.org", "code": "29463-7", "display": "Body weight" }]
  },
  "subject": { "reference": "Patient/{{patientId}}" },
  "valueQuantity": { "value": 70, "unit": "kg", "system": "http://unitsofmeasure.org", "code": "kg" }
}

### Preview the exact Patient filter before deleting (one match after seeding)
GET {{baseUrl}}/Patient?_id={{patientId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Scenario A: type-level soft delete with search filtering
# Expected: 202, empty body, absolute Content-Location header.
# @name softDelete
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/Patient/$bulk-delete?_id={{patientId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}
Prefer: respond-async

### Poll soft delete: 202 while running, then 200 with Patient count 1
GET {{softDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Verify soft delete: 410 Gone (the Observation is not deleted)
GET {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Verify soft delete retains Patient history, including a deletion entry
GET {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}/_history
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Scenario B: hard delete (re-run seed PUTs first; SQL storage required)
# Physically removes every version of the matched Patient.
# @name hardDelete
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/Patient/$bulk-delete?_id={{patientId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}&_hardDelete=true
Prefer: respond-async

### Poll hard delete: 202 while running, then 200 with Patient count 1
GET {{hardDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Verify hard delete: 404 Not Found
GET {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Scenario C: purge history (re-run seed PUTs first; SQL storage required)
# Keeps the current Patient version and removes its historical versions.
# @name purgeHistory
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/Patient/$bulk-delete?_id={{patientId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}&_purgeHistory=true
Prefer: respond-async

### Poll history purge: 202 while running, then 200 with Patient count 1
GET {{purgeHistory.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Verify history purge: 200 with only the current Patient version in history
GET {{baseUrl}}/Patient/{{patientId}}/_history
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Scenario D: system-level hard delete using a Parameters body (re-seed first)
# Only the two tagged sample resources are targeted, not all Patient/Observation data.
# Ignixa honors these body flags; fhir-server reads flags only from the query.
# hardDelete takes precedence when both flags are true. SQL storage required.
# @name systemDelete
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/$bulk-delete?_type=Patient,Observation&_id={{patientId}},{{observationId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}
Prefer: respond-async
Content-Type: application/fhir+json

{
  "resourceType": "Parameters",
  "parameter": [
    { "name": "hardDelete", "valueBoolean": true },
    { "name": "purgeHistory", "valueBoolean": true }
  ]
}

### Poll system delete: 200 on completion, with Patient and Observation counts
GET {{systemDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Optional: cancel Scenario D immediately after kickoff, instead of polling
# Expected: 202 when cancellation is accepted, or 409 if already terminal.
# Two sample resources may finish before cancellation arrives. Cancellation does
# not undo deletions; poll the same URL for partial counts and "Job Canceled".
DELETE {{systemDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}

### Scenario E: exclude Observation from a system-level soft delete (re-seed first)
# @name excludedTypesDelete
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/$bulk-delete?_type=Patient,Observation&_id={{patientId}},{{observationId}}&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}&excludedResourceTypes=Observation
Prefer: respond-async

### Poll excluded-types delete: only Patient appears in the completed counts
GET {{excludedTypesDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Verify the excluded Observation remains: 200 OK
GET {{baseUrl}}/Observation/{{observationId}}
Accept: application/fhir+json

### Scenario F: no matches still creates a successful asynchronous job
# @name noMatchDelete
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/Patient/$bulk-delete?_id={{patientId}}-missing&_tag={{tagSystem}}%7C{{tagCode}}
Prefer: respond-async

### Poll no-match delete: 200 with {"resourceType":"Parameters"} and no counts
GET {{noMatchDelete.response.headers.Content-Location}}
Accept: application/fhir+json
